>dfz@cypress14.cray.com (Dan Zimmerman) writes:
>
>I was just looking at the spoilers last night and the monster intrinsics
>section lists "acid" as one of the intrinsics a player is able to obtain
>from eating a monster corpse.  Does this mean acid resistant?

   I don't have a copy of the spoiler file now but if so this is incorrect.

>There is an unexplained game message that says "You develop a bad case of
>stomach acid."  Does this mean acid resistance acquired?

   Nope. This merely indicates you've eaten an acid monster - usually with
an associated loss of hit points (NOT strength). Try eating another acid
monster corpse and you'll get this message again. This couldn't happen if
you'd acquired an intrinsic.

>Or is this truly an intrinsic a player cannot get, like stone resistance,
>which I would like to be acquireable; hey, why not?

   The way NetHack handles this is rather confusing. Acid based monsters
(ochre jelly, yellow dragon etc.) are immune to acid attack. If you
polymorph into an acid monster you get immunity to attacks by ochre
jellies and one can eat acid monsters without hit point loss.
However, you *don't* get immunity from acid blasts from yellow dragons
(even if polymorphed *into* a yellow dragon). Also checking intrinsics
when polymorphed into an acid based monster shows no acid resistance
intrinsic. Furthermore, wearing yellow dragon scale mail gives no
immunity to *any* type of acid damage. One must conclude that there is no
intrinsic acid resistance in the game (although some monsters have
immunity. The developers seem to have thought enough about this to include
it in the game to an extent, but in an incomplete way.

